1) internal/tui/archive_delete_confirm_theme_test.go, func TestArchiveConfirmTokensMatchSpec
   guard: if keyHex == hintHex { t.Skip("this theme's key and hint tokens share a colour; the prose-vs-key distinctness assertion below would be vacuous") }
   reason: the test asserts the footer legend's bound-key word renders in theme.Key while the
   surrounding prose renders in theme.Hint, i.e. that the two tokens are visually distinct. If the
   active built-in theme happens to author theme.Key and theme.Hint at the identical hex value,
   that specific assertion has nothing to distinguish -- it would pass or fail on a tautology, not
   on any real rendering behaviour. The test still runs and asserts the title/dimmed/key-vs-prose
   assertions that do not depend on that coincidence; only the one now-vacuous comparison is
   skipped, and only when the coincidence is actually present in the running theme.

2) internal/tui/archive_delete_confirm_theme_test.go, func TestDeleteConfirmTokensMatchSpec
   same guard, same reason, same file (a second dialog covered by the same new test file).

3) internal/tui/bulk_delete_theme_test.go, func TestBulkDeleteConfirmTokensMatchSpec
   same guard, same reason (bulk-delete confirm dialog).

4) internal/tui/env_editor_theme_test.go, func TestEnvViewClosingHintKeysAreKeyToken
   guard: if keyHex == hintHex { t.Skip("this theme's key and hint tokens happen to share a colour; the distinctness assertion below would be vacuous") }
